A||||8||HummelHund||Fixed one thing, now it's another!!!||||5||
Z||000000||HummelHund||04-30-2001||06:37 PM||||Well, I've fixed my stumbling problem on my '84 SE 2M4 by adjusting the timing, which was about an inch off the scale. This might have been the problem with the idle as well, I haven't had a chance to let it cool off so I can check it when it's stone cold. But another problem has reared its ugly head: every once and awhile, usually about 10 minutes into a drive, the car will cut off as I am braking to a stop. This happens when my feet are on clutch and brake and when I'm almost stopped. What could be wrong this time? TPS? Ignition module? Any help would be appreciated.<BR><P>------------------<BR>Sean W.<BR>'84 SE 2M4 WS6 (restoration project to become daily driver)<BR>'84 Sport Coupe ($150 running car)||||reg||5
Z||000001||StuGood||04-30-2001||07:31 PM||||Well, what did you expect for $150, anyway    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">??<P>I really don't know, but since it's a 1984 model, the first thing I would suspect is the clutch not fully releasing.  Next time it happens, try shifting from 1st to reverse - if you get a "buzz-t!," then you'll know that's it.<P>BTW, I don't own a 1984 Fiero, but from what I have read, they seem to tend to have clutch problems, I believe.      <P>Hmmm... I think I like PBJ's answer better!: <BR><p>[This message has been edited by StuGood (edited 04-30-2001).]||||reg||6
Z||000002||PBJ||04-30-2001||08:10 PM||||Sounds like it may be an idle problem. Doesn't the computer take over idle after goes into closed loop. approx 5-10 min after cold start up.  Suggest remove IAC (idle air control valve) from throttle body and see if it is siezed-cleaning port in tbi is a good idea too.  ????||||reg||1
Z||000003||HummelHund||04-30-2001||09:56 PM||||How do you check to see if the IAC is seized? The IAC is one of the many things I replaced in my quest to find a stable idle. Could it be that I got the wrong IAC? I have heard that the '84 IAC is different from other years, and I bought mine at Autozone(it was a Wells, I think). Should I have ordered it from the Fiero Store or gotten the AC Delco? If so does anyone know what the part # for the AC Delco IAC is?<BR><P>------------------<BR>Sean W.<BR>'84 SE 2M4 WS6 (restoration project to become daily driver)<BR>'84 Sport Coupe ($150 running car)||||reg||5
Z||000004||HummelHund||04-30-2001||09:58 PM||||One thing I forgot to mention, when the car quits and I am trying to restart it, it will not restart unless I floor the gas pedal. Does this narrow it down any?<BR><P>------------------<BR>Sean W.<BR>'84 SE 2M4 WS6 (restoration project to become daily driver)<BR>'84 Sport Coupe ($150 running car)||||reg||1
Z||000005||PBJ||04-30-2001||10:00 PM||||You can tell if your IAC is siezed by removing it and trying to push it in 1/8 of an inch-if it moves it is not sized. BUT dont try and push it in or pull it out all the way or you could jam it up.||||reg||1
Z||000006||PBJ||04-30-2001||10:04 PM||||yes I narrows down the problem with your foot to the floor you have put the computer fuel system into clear flood mode.  So now I would say it sounds like you are getting to much fuel at idle. possibly a leaking/sticky injector.  May want to try injector cleaner?||||reg||5
Z||000007||HummelHund||04-30-2001||10:10 PM||||I've already taken apart my TBI and cleaned everything, replaced gaskets and o-rings. I've tried injector cleaner, and I've replaced that tiny filter on the injector itself. I think there's a test in the Haynes manual to see if it's leaking, I'll try that tomorrow. The manual says you can't rebuild the injector, so if it's bad I guess I'll have to buy a new one. Pretty soon I'll have a new car  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">. <BR><P>------------------<BR>Sean W.<BR>'84 SE 2M4 WS6 (restoration project to become daily driver)<BR>'84 Sport Coupe ($150 running car)||||reg||1
Z||000008||HummelHund||05-01-2001||04:18 PM||||Ok, here's the results after checking the IAC and the injector.<BR>The IAC does push in and pull out. I was careful not to do it too much, but it does move. The injector does not leak, I used the Haynes test (take off injector connector, turn ignition to ON and watch for drips). The fuel injector does sound a little wierd though, you can hear it when it sprays, and it's a little erratic. Does this mean the injector's sticking and therefore needs to be replaced? I'm getting my ignition module checked today at the local AutoZone to see if that has anything to do with it. Anything else I can check? Thanks for everyone's help, hopefully my Fiero will be on the road soon.<BR><P>------------------<BR>Sean W.<BR>'84 SE 2M4 WS6 (restoration project to become daily driver)<BR>'84 Sport Coupe ($150 running car)||||reg||1
